' THINKING MACHINES
' The four "Artificial Intelligence" programs of Tim Hartnell's Giant Book
' of Computer Games (Ballantine Books, 1984; Interface Publications, 1983),
' for the ColecoVision in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' ELIZA: your computer psychiatrist. Type a sentence; ELIZA looks for one
' of its 35 key words, answers with its reply and the rest of your
' sentence turned round (I becomes YOU, AM becomes ARE...), or with one of
' eleven general replies. BYE ends the session.
' ELECTRONIC BRAIN: think of three different digits, 1 to 9. The computer
' guesses; you tell it the blacks (right digit, right place) and whites
' (right digit, wrong place). It weighs the digits as the book's listing
' does until it cracks the code.
' PROBOSCIDEAN: concentration with 52 cards against the computer, which
' remembers every card it has seen (level 1) or forgets (level 10).
' AMANUENSIS: the computer writes poetry, one verse after another.
'
' Controls (either joystick): the stick moves over the keyboard, the
' cards or a number; the button types, picks or goes on. The second
' button leaves. Keypad not used.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - ELIZA: you type on a keyboard drawn on the screen (letters, the
'   apostrophe, space, DEL and END), up to 50 characters. When a key word
'   ends your sentence, nothing of it is repeated (the listing repeats the
'   whole sentence). A conjugated word is replaced in the rest of the
'   sentence (the listing's lines 370-420 lose letters around it).
' - ELECTRONIC BRAIN: the three-digit version. With two blacks the whites
'   are 0 (the listing keeps the last answer). A whites count is limited
'   to what can be true; a guess is never repeated; if no code fits your
'   answers (or after 21 guesses) the computer says so instead of
'   thinking forever.
' - PROBOSCIDEAN: the cards are drawn and chosen with the stick. The
'   computer searches all of its memory for a pair (the listing's line
'   250 only looks at its last card); it remembers up to 60 cards.
' - AMANUENSIS: the verses are typed on parchment, a new one every few
'   seconds (or at the button).
' - New pictures (ELIZA's monitor, the brain, the cards, the quill), new
'   music (Turing's Waltz) and sounds.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; at most eight
' sprites (the sparks of a win), two in play; every screen is drawn with
' the NMI off after a wait.

